<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Piar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Piar was a general of the Venezuelan war of independence, born in Curaçao. He led the patriot campaign in Guayana, winning the Battle of San Félix in 1817 and helping to secure Angostura as a base. Accused by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> of insubordination and of stirring racial conflict, he was court-martialled and shot at Angostura in October 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-piar.jpg" alt="Manuel Piar" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pablo W. Hernández (1890-1928) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Carlos_Piar_2012_000.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Piar was the son of Fernando Alonso Piar y Lottyn, a Spanish merchant seaman of Canarian origin, and María Isabel Gómez, a woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a> in Curaçao whose father was Afro-Venezuelan and whose mother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. As a quadroon he grew up under the limits that colonial society placed on people of mixed descent. He came to Venezuela with his mother at the age of ten and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-guaira">La Guaira</a>, where, without formal schooling, he taught himself several languages.</p><p>At 23 he took part in the unsuccessful Gual and España Conspiracy of 1797. In 1804 he served in the Curaçao militia that expelled the British occupiers and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule, and in 1807 he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, commanding a warship in support of the revolution there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1810 Piar served the Venezuelan rising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, first in the navy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>, where he fought the Spanish fleet in several engagements, among them the Battle of Sorondo on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> in 1812. After a spell of refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> he returned in 1813 as an army colonel, defended Maturín and helped free the east of the country. As a brigadier general in 1814 he fought in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumana">Cumaná</a>, and lost an engagement to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a> near El Salado.</p><p>Promoted to major general, he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> in the Los Cayos expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. In 1816 he defeated Francisco Tomás Morales at El Juncal and marched into Guayana, where his victory over Miguel de la Torre at San Félix was followed by his promotion to General-in-Chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Piar wanted independence and also greater social and political rights for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a>, and he was the only senior commander who was not a white criollo. After his victories he came into conflict with Bolívar, who stripped him of direct command; Piar took leave in June 1817 and stayed in Guayana to win support for his views. Other commanders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-marino">Santiago Mariño</a> and José Francisco Bermúdez, also opposed Bolívar, but Piar alone was arrested, on 28 September 1817, and it is generally agreed that Bolívar meant to make an example of him.</p><p>A court martial found him guilty of desertion, insubordination and conspiracy, and on 16 October 1817 he was shot against the wall of the cathedral of Angostura. Bolívar, who did not attend, is said to have heard the shots from his office and said in tears, &quot;He derramado mi sangre&quot; (I have spilled my blood).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Piar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1817 patriot forces under <b>Manuel Piar</b> and Bolívar captured the Guayana region and its town of Angostura, now Ciudad Bolívar, on the Orinoco, giving the patriots a secure base.</blockquote><a href="/state/third-republic-of-venezuela">Third Republic of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-bolivar">Ciudad Bolívar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.11,-68.94&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Willemstad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2656 1712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manuel Piar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3106 1982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3106 1982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.11,-68.94&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
